 "cushionNotice": "A cushion can also shrink: the D.C. Council shortened two kinds of paid leave in its FY2027 budget while keeping the payroll tax, and Congress then had its review period (Barrier 4). Yet the table by ward shows the same city delivering very different lives. A cushion on paper is not a cushion that reaches you: it must be known about, applied for, renewed, and located near where you live. Count the cushions, then ask who can actually reach each one. That is the fence we cannot see from inside.",
 "fences": [
  {
   "h": "Insulation",
   "text": "Most RJ families meet a lost job, an eviction notice or an arrest with a lawyer, savings, a relative's spare room, or a parent's paid leave. From inside those cushions, a man in a breakfast line looks like he made a choice."
  },
  {
   "h": "Physical: the monument city vs. the hometown city",
   "text": "The group sleeps eight blocks from the Capitol and spends days at the Mall's museums and on the House floor. Washington is built to be seen from the Mall, and the Metro carries visitors past the neighborhoods where most residents live. A week can pass without crossing the Anacostia. The shelter, the museums and the Capitol are a few blocks apart, so it is easy to feel you have seen D.C. when you have seen the part built for visitors."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive",
   "text": "D.C. means the government. Students arrive knowing the D.C. of civics class (three branches, how a bill becomes law) and almost nothing of the city's own history: Chocolate City, Home Rule, the 1968 corridors, the river. We also bring a picture of homelessness as addiction or bad choices. The data say most homeless people in D.C. are sheltered, many work, and nearly all are Black in a city that is 43% Black (Barrier 1). That is a structural pattern, not a moral one. Statehood arrives as a partisan slogan. Under it is a mechanism students can describe without taking a side (Barrier 4)."
  },
  {
   "h": "Affective",
   "text": "A short, warm conversation over breakfast can feel like the whole encounter; it is a beginning. Fear of men on the street, sharpened by news of crime operations, can decide what students notice. The museums' power (the Emmett Till exhibit, the Holocaust Museum) can make injustice feel like history, safely behind glass, a few blocks from a breakfast line that is not. Feeling good about serving can crowd out the harder question of why the line exists. Keep three things apart: what you saw, what you were told and by whom, and what you concluded."
  },
  {
   "h": "Theological",
   "text": "We're so blessed reads our comfort as God's favor. Seeing Jesus in every person, McKenna's own advice, can be misheard as turning a guest into a symbol for *our* spiritual experience, when he meant the opposite: the person is the point. Charity without justice, the bowl of oatmeal without the question of the rent, was exactly what McKenna refused. He fed people *and* built housing *and* argued with the powerful."
  }
 ],
